WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.780
A secure resource management baseline in C# starts with clarity.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:16.760
Identify what resources the application uses, who owns them, how long they should live, what releases them, and what limits prevent one feature from consuming capacity needed elsewhere.

3
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.840
Use IDisposable and IAsyncDisposable intentionally.

4
00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:33.780
Dispose or asynchronously dispose files, streams, handles, commands, readers, transactions, network-backed abstractions, and other owned resources according to their APIs.

5
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:40.040
Do not rely on garbage collection to release external capacity at the moment production needs it released.

6
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.280
Control the resources that cross service boundaries.

7
00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:56.340
Reuse network clients safely, scope database resources and transactions carefully, keep dependency injection lifetimes aligned with ownership, and make background work create and clean up the scopes it needs.

8
00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:03.020
Bound memory, buffers, caches, queues, fan-out, retries, and concurrency.

9
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:14.000
Pass cancellation where supported, set timeouts where waiting forever would be harmful, and design cleanup paths that leave application state consistent after failure or shutdown.

10
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.480
Finally, make resource behavior observable.

11
00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:26.380
Redact diagnostics, monitor production signals, review cleanup and recovery paths before release, and keep runbooks close to the systems they describe.

12
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:33.040
Secure resource management is practical engineering discipline applied before resource problems become incidents.
